I saw you mentioned aggregation. Aggregation should be a mode that applies to no matter which type of source and target is specified. 

In aggregated backup, We should employ an `Aggregator` into the origin pipeline to receive data from reader and aggregate them into blob files and push to writer. (Large files or special file like symlink that don't need aggregation can be passed to writer directly).

In aggregated copy restore, we similarly employ an `UnAggregator` into the origin pipeline, to receive FileControlBlock from control file reader and extract data from blobs to push to writer (for small files), and for large files, UnAggregator will directly push to reader to read from source repo.

Please assess if this idea is fesible and clear, if so, please optimize the code aggregation mode for all data source & target.














```
Findings

  1. nfs -> smb is not implemented.
     run_backup_subtask() in src/frame/subtask.rs has branches for:
      - local -> smb
      - smb -> local
      - smb -> smb
      - local/nfs combinations
        but there is no nfs -> smb branch. It falls into the SMB catch-all and returns "this SMB backup direction is not implemented yet".

     That matches your observed nfs -> smb failure.
  2. smb -> nfs is also not implemented.
     Same reason: there is no smb -> nfs branch in src/frame/subtask.rs.
     So this case should eventually fail too, but only after scan finishes.
  3. smb -> local and smb -> smb are implemented, but SMB-source scan is very slow and looks hung.
     The source-side copy paths do exist:
      - src/frame/backup_impls.rs: SmbSourceFileBackup, SmbSourceTargetFileBackup
      - src/backup/aio/directions.rs: run_smb_to_local_copy_pipeline(), run_smb_to_smb_copy_pipeline()

     The problem is Phase 2 scan:
      - src/frame/scanner_impls.rs runs SMB scan on a new_current_thread() Tokio runtime
      - src/smb/scanner.rs walks directories with a single local stack
      - it does open dir -> query_info -> Directory::query("*") one directory at a time
      - worker_count is effectively ignored for SMB traversal

     So for a large tree like ds2, SMB-source jobs spend a long time in scan before any copy starts. Since scan logs are routed to C_REPO/logs/scan.log,
     stdout looks quiet, so it feels stuck.
  4. smb -> nfs looks stuck for two reasons combined.
      - First it hits the same slow SMB scan bottleneck as above.
      - Then, after scan, it will fail because the actual smb -> nfs backup direction is not implemented.

  Why local/NFS cases behave better

  - Local scan is parallel/blocking-thread based.
  - NFS scan uses a dedicated async NFS scanner path in src/scanner.rs and is much better optimized than SMB.
  - SMB scan is currently the outlier.

  Practical summary by case

  - smb -> smb: implemented, but Phase 2 scan is very slow
  - smb -> local: implemented, but Phase 2 scan is very slow
  - smb -> nfs: not implemented; slow scan first, then failure
  - nfs -> smb: not implemented; fails in subtask phase

  How to confirm
  Check the backup copy’s scan log while one of the SMB-source jobs runs:

  tail -f <COPY_...>/C_REPO/logs/scan.log

  If it is progressing there, it is not deadlocked; it is just crawling through SMB scan.

  The next useful fix is clear: make SMB scan parallel like the local/NFS scanners, then add the missing mixed remote directions smb -> nfs and nfs -> smb.
 
