./target/release/fptcli backup --data "nfs://127.0.0.1/opt/dataset?sub=/test_smoke" --target "nfs://127.0.0.1/opt/dataset?sub=/out" --temp-dir /opt/target/work --format common --nfs-uid 1000 --nfs-gid 1000













• Yes. Based on the latest timing, remaining wins are likely in these areas:

  1. Tune file-task concurrency
      - We just changed default to 2 tasks / connection.
      - Next practical test is compare 2, 3, 4 tasks per connection, but make it configurable first, e.g. --smb-copy-tasks.
      - Risk: too much concurrency previously made SMB slower, so this should be measured, not hard-coded blindly.
  2. Avoid or reduce per-file open cost
      - Your latest run still had noticeable source_open and target_open time.
      - For many-small-file workloads, open latency can dominate.
      - Possible improvements: batch tiny files into aggregate layout for SMB targets, or optimize directory/file creation path to avoid redundant parent
        handling.
  3. Separate SMB read/write connection pools
      - SMB->SMB already uses source and target pools, but both point to the same server/share in your test.
      - We can experiment with one source connection and one target connection versus shared/session reuse patterns if smb-rs supports it cleanly.
  4. Profile smb-rs
      - Use perf record on a longer SMB-source backup.
      - Need to determine whether time is in signing/encryption, async message dispatch, buffer copies, Samba wait, or our task scheduling.
      - This is important before deeper changes inside the SMB client dependency.
  5. Reduce memory copies in SMB streaming
      - Current read-ahead uses Vec<u8> buffers and truncation.
      - We can implement reusable double buffers to avoid allocation per read.
      - This is probably a smaller win than RPC/open latency, but clean and low risk.
  6. SMB scanner/backup shared directory metadata
      - If backup consumes control files from an SMB scan, some directory/path metadata is already known.
      - We might reuse more of that to reduce later target-side operations, especially for aggregate/common restore paths.

  My recommended next step: add configurable --smb-copy-tasks and test 1/2/4 with --smb-connections 1 --buffer-size 2048. That is the fastest way to find
  whether more latency hiding is available before doing lower-level smb-rs profiling.








